1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,767
Hello! Last time we learned the vowels.

2
00:00:02,767 --> 00:00:06,380
Today we meet the other hero of Hangeul: the consonants.

3
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:08,510
What shapes were they modeled on?

4
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:15,184
Amazingly,

5
00:00:15,184 --> 00:00:19,506
Hangeul consonants were modeled on the very organs that make sound: the mouth,

6
00:00:19,506 --> 00:00:21,002
the tongue, and the throat.

7
00:00:21,002 --> 00:00:22,720
This is the principle of shape.

8
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:30,964
First, "ㄱ".

9
00:00:30,964 --> 00:00:35,571
It shows the root of the tongue blocking the throat.

10
00:00:35,571 --> 00:00:40,000
Say the sound: '그'. It sounds near the back teeth.

11
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:47,675
"ㄴ" shows the tongue tip touching the upper gum.

12
00:00:47,675 --> 00:00:48,015
'느'.

13
00:00:48,015 --> 00:00:52,430
The tongue gently meets the front roof of the mouth.

14
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,978
"ㅁ" is the shape of closed lips. '므'.

15
00:00:57,978 --> 00:01:01,080
The two lips meet and close.

16
00:01:03,530 --> 00:01:07,196
"ㅅ" is the shape of a tooth. '스'.

17
00:01:07,196 --> 00:01:10,750
Air slips out between the teeth.

18
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:16,672
"ㅇ" is the round shape of the throat.

19
00:01:16,672 --> 00:01:20,284
As a final sound it becomes the nasal '응'.

20
00:01:20,284 --> 00:01:23,810
At the start, it is a silent placeholder.

21
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:33,483
So the five basic consonants — "ㄱ", "ㄴ", "ㅁ", "ㅅ",

22
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:38,690
"ㅇ" — were born from the shapes of our own body.

23
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:41,817
What about the rest?

24
00:01:41,817 --> 00:01:44,870
A very clever method: when the sound gets stronger,

25
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:47,205
you add one stroke to the basic letter.

26
00:01:47,205 --> 00:01:49,360
This is the stroke-adding principle.

27
00:01:57,330 --> 00:02:04,237
Add a stroke to "ㄴ" and you get "ㄷ"; add to "ㅁ" and you get "ㅂ".

28
00:02:04,237 --> 00:02:08,230
The sound becomes clearer and firmer.

29
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:15,870
Add a stroke to "ㅅ" for "ㅈ", and to "ㅇ" for "ㅎ".

30
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:21,880
And the rolling "ㄹ" too. Do you see the pattern?

31
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:32,667
Here are the ten basic consonants for today: "ㄱ",

32
00:02:32,667 --> 00:02:38,707
"ㄴ", "ㄷ", "ㄹ", "ㅁ", "ㅂ", "ㅅ", "ㅇ", "ㅈ", "ㅎ".

33
00:02:38,707 --> 00:02:43,100
Let's call their names together.

34
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,252
Now the most magical part!

35
00:02:46,252 --> 00:02:51,644
Hangeul does not write consonant and vowel separately — it gathers them into one block.

36
00:02:51,644 --> 00:02:53,380
This is 'syllable assembly'.

37
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:02,583
The consonant is the first sound,

38
00:03:02,583 --> 00:03:04,908
the vowel the middle sound.

39
00:03:04,908 --> 00:03:09,560
Put first "ㄱ" and middle "ㅏ" together and you get '가'.

40
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:22,415
The rule is simple. Upright vowels like ㅏ, ㅓ,

41
00:03:22,415 --> 00:03:29,210
ㅣ go to the right of the consonant — like 나, 머, 디.

42
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:38,328
In contrast, flat vowels like ㅗ, ㅜ,

43
00:03:38,328 --> 00:03:44,600
ㅡ go below the consonant — like 고, 누, 드.

44
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,541
Today we use only letters without a final consonant.

45
00:03:50,541 --> 00:03:51,441
With no final,

46
00:03:51,441 --> 00:03:54,140
the sound stays open — an 'open syllable'.

47
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,926
Let's build a word.

48
00:04:00,926 --> 00:04:05,849
Write '고' and '기' side by side: '고기' — meat.

49
00:04:05,849 --> 00:04:09,430
Two blocks join to make meaning!

50
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:21,029
Let's name family. 'ㄴ' and 'ㅏ' make '나', and '누나'.

51
00:04:21,029 --> 00:04:27,900
'오빠' and '누나' are warm words for family.

52
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:36,578
머리 means head. 바나나 means banana.

53
00:04:36,578 --> 00:04:41,290
The consonants and vowels snap together neatly!

54
00:04:57,370 --> 00:05:00,276
아버지 (father) and 어머니 (mother).

55
00:05:00,276 --> 00:05:03,181
Even with only open syllables,

56
00:05:03,181 --> 00:05:05,990
we can write such warm words.

57
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:11,019
오이 (cucumber), 호수 (lake),

58
00:05:11,019 --> 00:05:15,641
지도 (map) — easy open-syllable words.

59
00:05:15,641 --> 00:05:18,850
Read them aloud after me.

60
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:24,313
Now, flashcard time! '고기', '호수', '기차'.

61
00:05:24,313 --> 00:05:26,539
Read them out loud,

62
00:05:26,539 --> 00:05:30,640
recalling each consonant and vowel.

63
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:37,364
Try writing each consonant stroke by stroke with your pencil.

64
00:05:37,364 --> 00:05:40,380
Writing by hand helps you remember the shape and sound.

65
00:05:43,380 --> 00:05:46,946
Today we learned ten basic consonants and how to assemble blocks.

66
00:05:46,946 --> 00:05:49,140
Now you can read and write simple words!

67
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:58,112
Practice a little every day and your Hangeul will grow fast.

68
00:05:58,112 --> 00:06:00,672
Subscribe and like to join the next journey.

69
00:06:00,672 --> 00:06:01,720
See you next time!

